  "query_preview": "..\nFriday through Sunday: The upper level trough off coast continues to \nweaken and fill in, but will leave cool and unsettled conditions \npresent in the region through this weekend with temperatures near \naverage to slightly below average. Shower chances Friday will be \nlimited to the east slopes of the Cascades. Snow levels will be \naround 3000-4500 feet, so there is a small chance (10%) for 1\" \nor more at Washington Pass.\n\nThe forecast for Saturday evening looks drier than it was looking \nlast night but there are still a small chance for sprinkles (5-20% \nchance), mostly in central/eastern Washington and the southern ID \nPanhandle. Evening temperatures will fall into the low to mid 40s, \naccompanied by light northeast winds around 1-4 mph. The chance of \nseeing 0.25 inches or more of rain from 4 AM Saturday to 4 AM Monday \nhas drastically dropped to less than 5 percent outside of the lower \nPalouse and L-C valley. The chances are around 10-20% with the \nhighest on the Camas Prairie. \n\nShower chances Sunday will be limited to southeast Washington east \nof US-395 and the southern ID Panhandle as the trough moves ashore. \nThe best dynamics appear to be trending southward in the model runs \nso the chances of a quarter inch or more of rain through Monday \nmorning have dropped off significantly to less than 5 percent \noutside of the lower Palouse and L-C valley. The chances are around \n10-20% with the highest on the Camas Prairie. \n\nMonday through Thursday: Models are still in good agreement of a \nbuilding ridge Monday which will bring dry conditions and slightly \nabove normal temperatures in the low to mid 50s. High temperatures \ncould under perform if stratus/fog layers develop but \nconfidence is low at these lead times. Ensembles are trending \ntowards unsettled conditions returning towards late next week \n(into Thursday) with potential for showers and high mountain \nsnow. /DB",
